From 185fb661ad197d4cb3084ddcb91d4932e7b48d66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LiBr <me@nvme0n1p.dev>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:49:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 37/47] Z 6III: the still through engine 0x150c --
 z63-still-capture, and the capture models' timers

A release now runs engine 0x150c through a still: the units reset, stop
and start in this image's order, and the D980 writer puts the still's
Bayer frame in memory at the front's request. No file is written yet;
where the capture stops is below.

The z9 attachment never created two timers ../z9-qemu's machine init
does, the secondary-source timing and the D980 single-frame input, nor
wired the still engines' interrupts. With the capture recipe on (the
event1508-secondary-timing and primary-d980-single-frame models), the
first still that armed one dereferenced a null timer: QEMU took SIGSEGV
in timer_mod from a 0x1508 front write. Both timers are now created where
the Z 9 init creates them, and engines 0x1540 (INTID 0x131), 0x1544
(0xfd), 0x153 (251/252) and 0x15e2 (121) are wired. Each INTID is this
image's own name for the block -- RAW1, RAWRS, SFLT0/SFLT1, JPEG_0 in
hw/arm/nikon-z63-spi.h -- and nothing else on the board drives them.

z63-still-capture (off by default; z9lab_attach.c.in has the whole
story). ../z9-qemu's 0x150c rules, written for the Z 9, refused this
image's still in one of two ways depending on timing:
  - the bounded reset (0x150c0014 = 0xc7ff7f ... 0x4f04f) comes while live
    view still runs; the model wanted the source timing stopped. Admitted
    live: a reset needs no quiescence;
  - the live-view units' stop (0x304f, kick 0x787d) and the direct stops
    then find the units idle; idle stops complete at once;
  - the commit/enable of the still units 0x401e comes with 19BC's
    direct-stop cause still pending -- this image never acknowledges it
    (not in its +0x200 mask); the unit may start again.
The front then asks for the still frame (channel 0 mode 2 -> 3, source
bitmap 0x200) with the frame timing still running, where the Z 9 had
stopped it; the single-frame input now makes its one frame there, the
periodic D980 path stays quiet, each still re-arms the writer's done
status (group1 bit 26, never acknowledged here), and the upper unit's
frame end, +0x204 bit 6, comes with the frame (sub_40CAE2EC passes it to
the front end as 0x41D0005; the upper's +0x14 bit 0 is clear for this
job). DECLARED: when the still frame arrives, and its frame end with it.
The edits are import_z9lab.py section 8 and its new nikon-z9-d980.h pass.

z63-miss-log=on names the writes the models decline (and, for 0x150c's
control words, the model's admission state). z63-tools/plugins/z63ftrace.c
lists the first call of each of a set of functions after a trigger (here
the release), with a "new" mark for those never run before it: the tool
that found the frontier below.

Where the still stops. The release controller (0xA03D81940, steps through
stage bits 32 -> 1024 -> 0x8000 -> 0x10000 -> ...) builds the capture
request (sub_40F5D9B4, release type 0 = single frame) and the capture
service fills its parameters from settings: 0xA03D7C628 is shooting
setting 0x206E (8302), stored at 0xA03F1D16C, range 0..2, 0 in this
body. sub_40F5E708 returns 0 for it, so 0xA03D7C760 = 0 and the service
takes its other path (state 0xA03D7CFEC 0 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6, release stage
0x10000 waiting for 0x40C000E). That path completes only on the front
end's image event 4 (sub_41020C74 after the still job 0x422D0003 ends)
and the still job is never started on it. Set to 1 by hand (gdb stub,
a test only), the service sets ready bit 1, the 0x4401 combination in
sub_40F749EC starts the front end's still (sub_4102E1B0 ->
sub_410ACE1C(2) -> job 0x422D0003, units 2 then 8), the capture job
0x422D0001 starts 0x401e, the sensor exposes and reads out, and the
service waits in state 0xe (0xA03D7CFEC = 6) for that image event. What
0x206E is, and why its value decides, is the next question.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
